HST: Ctrl+Alt+letter stays a shortcut on Windows layouts whose AltGr level repeats the letter

Ctrl+Alt is AltGr on Windows. The built-in Vietnamese keyboard layout gives a character on the AltGr
level of every key, and for the letters, comma and period it is the key's own character again
(Ctrl+Alt+Q gives "q"). vcl then dropped Ctrl and Alt and typed the letter, so every Ctrl+Alt+letter
shortcut (Ctrl+Alt+Q new equation, Ctrl+Alt+M comment, Ctrl+Alt+V paste special, Ctrl+Alt+, and . for
document tabs, ...) typed text for users of that layout.

Before the AltGr character wins, the key's character in the active layout without Ctrl and Alt is
looked up; when the key is a letter, comma or period and both characters are the same, the press
stays Ctrl+Alt+key, as on a layout without AltGr. Nothing typeable is lost: the key alone gives that
character. Digits keep their AltGr character (on that layout AltGr+digit is how digits are typed),
and real AltGr characters ("@" on German, Polish letters, "€") are typed as before.

The rule is in vcl/inc/hst/altgrsamechar.hxx, the layout lookup in vcl/win/hst/altgrsamechar.cxx
(HST-owned); salframe.cxx calls it before UnsetAltIfAltGr.

Test: CppunitTest_vcl_hst_altgr_same_char.

